Implement the power function pow(x, n) , which calculates the x raised to n i.e. xn.
Note : In output print 4 digits places after decimal point.

Example 1
Input : x = 2.0000 , n = 10
Output : 1024.0000
Explanation : Answer = 2^10 => 1024.

Example 2
Input : x = 2.0000 , n = -2
Output : 0.2500
Explanation : Answer = 2^(-2) = 1/4 => 0.25.

Constraints:-
-100.0 <= x <= 100.0
-2^31 <= n <= 2^31 - 1
-10^4 <= x^n <= 10^4
Either x is not zero or n>0.
n is an integer.

//code
class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int n) {
          long N=n;
          if(n<0){
            N=-N;
            x=1/x;
          }
          return po(x,n);
    }
    public double po(double x,int n){
        if(n==0)
            return 1;
        double val=po(x,n/2);
        if(n%2==0)
            return val*val;
        else
            return x*val*val;
    }
}
